    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d191">

                <pb facs="0298" n="203" sameAs="frus:frus1936v04/pg203"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.943 Manchuria/23: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-06-11T18:00:00+09:00">June 11, 1936—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received June 11—9:08 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>125. Treaty between Japan and Manchukuo signed yesterday is published in the
                    English press today.<anchor n="25" xml:id="d191-f1"/> The Foreign Office has
                    confirmed the accuracy of the text which is described as the first step towards
                    abolition of extraterritoriality in Manchuria. This treaty is concerned
                    primarily with questions arising out of the administration of the South
                    Manchuria Railway zone and the payment by Japanese of Manchukuo taxes. It
                    contemplates the withdrawal of special Japanese police by the end of 1937 and is
                    to be followed in due course by other agreements looking to the extension of
                    Manchukuo judicial authority over Japanese subjects resident in Manchuria. Text
                    follows by mail.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Peiping by mail.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d191-f1" n="25" xml:id="d191-f1-content">
                <p>For text of treaty signed at Hsinking, June 10, 1936, see <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >The American Journal of International Law</hi>, vol. 30, No. 4 (October
                    1936, Supplement), p. 188.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
